<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Curthose</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1051–<a href="/years/1134">1134</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Robert II, Duke of Normandy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Curthose was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> and duke of Normandy from 1087. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, pledging Normandy to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> to pay for it. Returning in 1100, he failed to win the English crown from his younger brother Henry I, who defeated him at Tinchebray in 1106 and kept him prisoner until his death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> in 1134.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-curthose.jpg" alt="Robert Curthose" width="286" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Curthose_-_MS_Royal_14_B_VI.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders">Matilda of Flanders</a>. His birth has been dated to between 1051 and 1053. The epithet Curthose comes from the Norman French courtheuse, 'short stockings'; the chroniclers William of Malmesbury and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a> wrote that his father derisively called him brevis-ocrea, 'short boot'. In 1063 his father made him Count of Maine, in view of his betrothal to Margaret, the heiress of Maine, who died before they could marry. In 1077 a quarrel with his younger brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> and Henry led him to attempt to seize Rouen Castle, and he spent the following years in exile, at one point wounding his father in battle in January 1079.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When William died in 1087 he gave Normandy to Robert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a>, and Henry received money to buy land. Robert was generous to his barons and the clergy but was almost perpetually short of funds, and the central authority of the duchy weakened. William Rufus bribed many of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> away. Robert's support for the Rebellion of 1088 against Rufus failed, and he never joined the English rebels himself.</p><p>In 1096 Robert left to join the First Crusade, mortgaging his duchy to Rufus for 10,000 marks. He fought at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> on 1 July 1097, took part in the siege and battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and was among the crusaders who captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 15 July 1099. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> on 12 August 1099 he commanded part of the crusader centre. On the way home he married Sybil of Conversano, whose dowry helped him buy back his duchy.</p><p>Rufus died on 2 August 1100 while Robert was still returning, and Henry seized the English crown. In 1101 Robert landed at Portsmouth with a fleet, but he and Henry agreed the Treaty of Alton, by which Robert recognised Henry's crown in exchange for 3,000 livres. His position in Normandy then deteriorated amid private wars among the barons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1106 Henry decisively defeated Robert's army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tinchebray">Battle of Tinchebray</a> and claimed Normandy for the English crown, a situation that lasted almost a century. Robert was captured and held at Corfe Castle, then at Devizes Castle and finally at Cardiff Castle, where he died probably on 3 February 1134. He was buried at Gloucester Abbey, where an effigy of bog oak, carved about a century after his death, lies on a mortuary chest. His reputation rests largely on his conduct on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, described as the one period of clear distinction in his career.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Curthose” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later dukes fought for the duchy itself: Henry I of England defeated his brother <b>Robert Curthose</b> at the Battle of Tinchebray in 1106 and Louis VI of France at the Battle of Brémule in 1119.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Forces loyal to the English king William Rufus tried and failed to take control of Rouen, capital of his elder brother <b>Robert Curthose</b>, Duke of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-3">3 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He put down rebellions by barons who favoured his elder brother <b>Robert Curthose</b>, and in 1096 took Normandy in pledge from Robert.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-26">26 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In Normandy, King Henry I of England defeated the Norman army of his elder brother <b>Robert Curthose</b>, Duke of Normandy, and captured him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-28">28 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Duchy of Normandy. Died in Cardiff Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.48,-3.18&amp;year=1104" aria-label="Cardiff Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4478 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Robert Curthose"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4928 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4928 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.48,-3.18&amp;year=1104">What was here in 1104? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
